  "notes": "Memphis's first major-label signing and a complete self-producer — he \"wrote, produced, and played all of the instruments\" on the early releases, and he is the son of Stax house drummer Willie Hall. That single fact is the whole model: **this is the played entry in a programmed lane.** It shows up in three places. (1) The loosest grid here — `quantizeStrength` 0.62, `velocityVar` 0.34, the largest `timingJitterMs` in this batch, wide velocity tiers, and snare ghosts at 0.45 across four positions, which is a drummer's feel rather than a grid. (2) Real harmony: i–iv–VII and i–VI–III–VII families, `maxChords` 4, sevenths at 0.33, `topVoiceStepwise` true, and a scale set that reaches `blues` for the blues-third and blues-seventh colour the research names. (3) A bass that moves with the changes — `independent_riff` at `followRootsProb` 0.5 with four passing tones over a 22-semitone register, because the bass is played rather than pedalled. His song forms are verse-hook-verse rather than loop-and-extend, so the arrangement is the most conventional of the tape-era entries, and the countermelody is genuinely present between vocal phrases (0.55, `fillGapsOnly`). ⛔ `low` because the research rates it medium-low and flags the drum feel itself: the \"played all the instruments\", label and lineage facts are sourced, **and the ghost-note/pushed-16th feel, the harmonic specifics and the 132–160 straight tempo band are reasoned inference from the multi-instrumentalist practice and the family lineage** — the research marks that drum row [LOW-CONF] explicitly. ⚠ The chord voicing moved up to [51, 81] so the bass stays under the harmony — it started at 44 against a bass reaching 50, an overlap of 7. The span is preserved; a voicing that low was competing with the bass rather than sitting over it.",
